Good to see some discussion on alt.fan.unabomber again.  Not that i agree
with either of you.

By merely expressing the notion that machines can possibly take over
from people, one shows that one has been brainwashed by the Ultimate
Propaganda Machine run by the press & Big Science & Technology.

I'm not sure even John McCarthy, the grandaddy of Artificial
Intelligence, who used to show up on this newsgroup before the
Slimeballs came along, would buy into that one.

AI, robots, & machines are nowhere near up to the task of taking
over society.  They can barely make it across the surface of an
extremely limited and uncomplicated space without bumping into
obstacles.  They can't even win chess competitions without a team of
human chess experts to program and tweak them between games.  And the REAL
DEBATE, which we used to engage in here, is concerned with whether they ever
will be able to do either.

My take on Ted is that he just may have realized how totally people have
been brainwashed by the idea that machines COULD take over.  Merely
believing in this moves an absurd idea a few steps closer to being
possible.  This is really dangerous enough.  The whole idea in itself is
almost enough to drive people crazy, let alone attaching any possible
reality to it.

I argue this in some detail on my website in my pieces on the
Sci-Tech Creed and automatic chauffeurs, both originally posted here
during our discussion with John McCarthy.

And also in my satire on AI entited "AE--Artificial Eroticism."

